#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125553} :activity/id #uuid "67f5c7b4-d6b9-56a1-a476-c05d63882f58" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049390 :topic/id #uuid "f0e26f7e-2461-5845-b570-4028c1709f5a" :topic/math-academy-id 1948} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125845 :tutorial/content "Suppose we would like to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2=1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/39/39893af9755488d142116370d451b7a3d92b334c27f983eb302492b0d516c41c.png)\n\nFirst, let's draw our constraint circle ($x^2+y^2=1$) and consider the level curves of $f(x,y).$ These level curves are ellipses of the form\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2 = k.\n$$\n\nAs we increase the parameter $k,$ we get ellipses with larger and larger axes, as shown below. \n\n![](images/d5/d59d566c9cf7e252884a1397eb4ac0c8f85b290790e9063be2ff6eb4a4aff04a.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $k,$ a level curve will touch the circle at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ for some value of $k.$ The value of $k$ corresponding to that level curve gives us the *smallest* possible value of $f(x,y)$ on our circle $x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nIncreasing $k$ even further, we'll get another value of $k$ for which the corresponding level curve touches the circle at the points $(0, \\pm 1),$ as shown below. This value of $k$ gives us the *largest* possible value of the function $f(x,y)$ on our circle.\n\n![](images/48/48f71f5aa237936be24ccba572fb650b0d477c252d8338549a33cfea4874cbad.png)\n\nNotice that the corresponding level curves touch the circle in both cases (the minimum and the maximum). So, the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ and the circle $g(x,y)=1$ have a common tangent at these points. This means that the *gradients* of both curves must be parallel at these points. Therefore, at the extreme points, we must have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is some real number. The number $\\lambda$ is called a **Lagrange multiplier**.\n\nWe can now use these observations to write down a general method for optimizing a function subject to a constraint." :tutorial/id #uuid "932667c3-77f7-56cb-ae08-dd6835e29462" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5109 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "639b5b29-18f2-5454-b193-c76dd6a45f3a" :step/math-academy-id 32055 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124421 :question/id #uuid "dc39fdca-dfbc-5806-b0aa-8480ee44b10f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8689"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372202}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"} :step/id #uuid "ba4c5cbe-9bb4-5323-8a0f-c6fcc06d7cc5" :step/math-academy-id 32291 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124424 :question/id #uuid "ebdcad27-1ee2-57e7-9ebf-29ccf0a8ad12" :question/math-academy-id "e-8736"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5328ab3-440f-5cc8-9b8f-e7f86a4dd740" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435021}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Three-Variable Function"} :step/id #uuid "242a9cf3-c2e5-570a-b9f0-936162411df1" :step/math-academy-id 32494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124208 :question/id #uuid "5c276425-f5ef-585f-9ab3-1b0262fff08e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12456"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435022}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"} :step/id #uuid "6e09518a-865f-5121-9684-78bd4bfcab56" :step/math-academy-id 44830 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125872 :tutorial/content "The **method of Lagrange multipliers** states that the extrema of a function $f(x,y)$ subject to constraint $g(x,y)=c$ can be found by solving the system\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curve $g(x,y)=c.$\n\nNote that solutions to this system do not always exist! For example, the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ does not have a minimum (or maximum) on the hyperbola $x^2-y^2=1.$\n\nHowever, we can use the following theorem to guarantee the existence of solutions in some cases:\n\n*If $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint function $g(x,y) = c$ is closed and bounded, then $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "906426bb-122c-5b43-a304-d63921ab20dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7750 :tutorial/title "The Method of Lagrange Multipliers"} :step/id #uuid "0d14ea94-0983-5096-b7d2-ed0dc8de4dce" :step/math-academy-id 44868 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125894 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider our previous example, where we wish to find the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x^2+4y^2$ on the circle $g(x,y)=x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $2$ at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at points $(0, \\pm 1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a5ffe247-960a-5ed1-92ae-ae8c917cbefc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7881 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "66d693a8-cc66-5185-8b5f-4ba65d4664e2" :step/math-academy-id 45437 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125555}}] :activity/title "Lagrange Multipliers With One Constraint" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124738}]}
